@node Overview
@chapter Overview
@cindex overview

Dials shows how busy a GNU/Linux system is, live, in a full-screen
terminal interface in the style of the classic two-panel file
managers.  Each subsystem has its own page: an overview, the
processors, memory, graphics processors, disks, network, processes,
hardware sensors and power.

Dials reads its measurements from @file{/proc} and @file{/sys}.  It
keeps the files it reads open and rereads them in place, so it uses
very little of the processor time it measures.

Dials needs a terminal of at least 80 columns and 24 lines to show a
page comfortably; on a smaller one, parts of a page are left out.  A
terminal and locale with UTF-8 give the best lines and bars; with
others, Dials draws with plain characters.  With @option{--once},
Dials prints a plain report instead, which suits scripts and remote
sessions.

This is an early version, and its pages may still change.

@node Invoking dials
@chapter Invoking @command{dials}
@cindex invoking
@cindex options

@example
dials [@var{option}]@dots{}
@end example

@table @option
@item -d
@itemx --demo
@opindex --demo
Show simulated data instead of this system's.  This is useful for
trying the interface and for screenshots.

@item -i @var{seconds}
@itemx --interval=@var{seconds}
@opindex --interval
Take a sample every @var{seconds}, which may be a fraction from 0.1 to
3600.  The default is 1.  Either @samp{.} or the locale's decimal
point may be used, as in @samp{0.5} or @samp{0,5}.  While Dials runs,
@kbd{-} and @kbd{+} step through 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 3, 5, 10, 30 and 60
seconds.

@item -p @var{name}
@itemx --page=@var{name}
@opindex --page
Start on page @var{name}: one of @samp{overview}, @samp{cpu},
@samp{memory}, @samp{gpu}, @samp{disk}, @samp{network},
@samp{processes}, @samp{sensors} or @samp{power}.

@item -t @var{name}
@itemx --theme=@var{name}
@opindex --theme
Use the color theme @var{name}: @samp{classic} (white on dark navy, the
default), @samp{blue} (white on the terminal's blue), @samp{dark} or
@samp{mono}.  Terminals without color always use @samp{mono}.

@item --log=@var{file}
@opindex --log
Write each sample to @var{file} from the start.  @xref{Logging}.

@item --once
@opindex --once
Print one plain-text report on standard output and exit, without
starting the full-screen interface.  Dials first takes two samples one
interval apart, so that rates such as processor use can be computed.
The report begins like this:

@example
Host:                  demo
System:                Demo System 1.0
Kernel:                6.0.0-demo x86_64
Processor:             Demo CPU 16-Core Processor
CPUs:                  16
Uptime:                3 days, 04:12
Load:                  1.32 0.35 0.13
CPU:                   33% (user 24%, system 6%, I/O wait 1%)
Memory:                10.1 / 32.0 GiB (31%), 21.9 GiB available
@end example

@noindent
and goes on with each GPU, disk, file system, network interface,
battery and power supply, and a summary of the sensors.  The labels
are translated and the numbers follow the locale, so programs that
read measurements should use a log instead (@pxref{Logging}).

@item --sysroot=@var{dir}
@opindex --sysroot
Read @file{/proc} and @file{/sys} under @var{dir} instead of the
running system's.  This is meant for testing with a saved copy of
those files.  File system space and GPUs are not measured then, since
they do not come from those files.

@item --help
@opindex --help
Print a summary of the options and exit.

@item --version
@opindex --version
Print the version number and licensing information and exit.
@end table

@menu
* Exit status::         What the exit status means.
* Environment::         Environment variables that Dials uses.
* Privileges::          What needs root privileges.
@end menu

@node Exit status
@section Exit status
@cindex exit status

Dials exits with status 0 when it ends normally, and with status 1
when an option is wrong, when the system's measurements cannot be
read, when the terminal cannot be used, or when its output cannot be
written.

@node Environment
@section Environment
@cindex environment variables

@table @env
@item TERM
The terminal type, which tells Dials how to draw and how many colors
it may use.  With fewer than eight colors, the @samp{mono} theme is
used.

@item LANG
@itemx LC_ALL
@itemx LC_MESSAGES
@itemx LC_NUMERIC
@itemx LC_CTYPE
The language of messages and labels, the decimal point of the numbers
shown, and whether the terminal uses UTF-8.  Files that Dials writes
for programs, such as logs and the settings file, always use
@samp{.} as the decimal point.

@item XDG_CONFIG_HOME
@itemx HOME
Where the settings file is.  @xref{Settings}.
@end table

@node Privileges
@section Privileges
@cindex root privileges
@cindex privileges

Dials needs no special privileges, and it is best run as an ordinary
user.  A few things depend on them:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Most systems let only root read processor power from RAPL
(@pxref{Power page}).
@item
Signals can be sent, and nice values raised, only for your own
processes; lowering a nice value needs root privileges
(@pxref{Processes page}).
@end itemize

@node The screen
@chapter The screen
@cindex screen layout

The top line is the menu bar.  Press @kbd{F9} to open it; the right
end shows the current page, whether sampling is live, paused or
simulated, the sampling interval and the time.

The bottom line lists what the function keys do.

Between them is the current page, or two pages side by side: press
@kbd{s} to show two panels or one.  Keys act on the active panel,
whose title is highlighted; @key{TAB} makes the other panel active
and @kbd{C-u} swaps them.  The page keys choose the active panel's
page.  On a screen narrower than 80 columns only the active panel is
shown.

@cindex menus
The menu bar has three menus.  @samp{Page} chooses the active panel's
page.  @samp{Options} pauses sampling, makes it less or more frequent,
clears the history, changes the color theme, shows one panel or two,
starts or stops logging, saves the setup (@pxref{Settings}), makes the
other panel active, swaps the panels and quits.  @samp{Help} lists the
keys and tells which version of Dials is running.

@cindex graphs
@cindex colors
Graphs scroll from right to left,
with the newest sample on the right; the label on the bottom of a
graph tells how much time it spans.  Bars and graphs are green below
60% of their limit, yellow below 85% and red above.

@node Pages
@chapter Pages
@cindex pages

@menu
* Overview page::       The whole system at a glance.
* CPU page::            Processors.
* Memory page::         Memory and swap.
* GPU page::            Graphics processors.
* Disks page::          Disks and file systems.
* Network page::        Interfaces and connections.
* Processes page::      The running processes.
* Sensors page::        Temperatures, fans and voltages.
* Power page::          Batteries and power use.
@end menu

@node Overview page
@section The Overview page
@cindex Overview page

The top of the page names the host, the operating system, the kernel
and the processor, with the uptime, the load averages, the number of
processors and the sampling interval.

Below are meters for processor, memory and swap use, and for the
utilization and video memory of up to two graphics processors.  At
the bottom, graphs show the recent history of processor and memory
use.

@node CPU page
@section The CPU page
@cindex CPU page
@cindex processors

The top of the CPU page names the processor and gives the number of
logical processors, their mean and highest current frequency, the
highest frequency the hardware allows, and the package temperature.
Below are the load averages, the share of time that tasks waited for
a processor in the last ten seconds, the number of tasks running and
blocked, and the rates of context switches, interrupts and new
processes.

A bar shows how the processors' time was used: by programs
(@samp{user} and @samp{nice}), by the kernel (@samp{system} and
@samp{IRQ}), waiting for input and output, and taken by a hypervisor
(@samp{steal}).

Then comes one cell per processor, with its use and, when there is
room, a bar and its frequency.  On machines with many processors the
cells get smaller to fit the screen.  At the bottom, graphs show the
history of the total use and of the temperature, or of the frequency
where the temperature is not known.

The temperature comes from the first of these hardware monitoring
drivers found: @code{coretemp}, @code{k10temp}, @code{zenpower},
@code{cpu_thermal}, @code{soc_thermal} and @code{acpitz}.

@node Memory page
@section The Memory page
@cindex Memory page
@cindex swap
@cindex zram

The first bar divides memory between programs, the kernel, buffers,
the page cache and free memory; the second shows how much swap is
used.  The figure at the end of the first bar is the memory in use:
the total less what is available, as the kernel estimates it.

Below are the details: shared memory, the parts of the kernel's own
use, the swap cache, the data stored in zram devices and the memory
it takes compressed, dirty pages and those being written back, the
memory committed to programs against the kernel's limit, and huge
pages.  On a wide screen a third column gives the rates of page
faults and of swapping, and the share of time that tasks were delayed
(@samp{Waiting}) or all stopped (@samp{Stalled}) for lack of memory.

Graphs show the history of memory and swap use, and a table lists the
ten processes with the most memory.

@node GPU page
@section The GPU page
@cindex GPU page
@cindex graphics processors
@cindex NVIDIA

The GPU page shows graphics processors from AMD, Intel and NVIDIA,
read from the kernel's drivers: @code{amdgpu} gives the most, while
@code{i915} and @code{xe} give the frequency and, from the time the
GPU spent idle, how busy it was, and @code{nouveau} gives what it
can.

@cindex NVML
NVIDIA's own driver is not free software, and neither is NVML, the
monitoring library that comes with it.  GPUs that use that driver are
shown only if Dials was configured with @option{--enable-nvml}; it
then loads NVML when it starts, if it is installed.  Names come from the PCI ID database, when it is
installed.  The page says why when there is nothing to show.

The list at the top has one line per GPU, with its temperature,
utilization, video memory, power, fan speed and a status.  The status
warns when a GPU reaches 85@tie{}@textdegree{}C or uses 95% of its
video memory.  Choose a GPU with @key{UP} and @key{DOWN}.

Below the list are the details of the chosen GPU: the driver version,
the performance state, the processor and memory clocks, the fan
speed and the use of the video encoder and decoder.  Then come blocks
with the current temperature, utilization, video memory and power,
each with the lowest, mean and highest value seen, graphs of their
history, and the processes using the GPU with their share of its time
and of its memory.  Processes are listed only for GPUs read through
NVML.

On a small screen the blocks are left out first, then the processes.

@node Disks page
@section The Disks page
@cindex Disks page
@cindex file systems

The list at the top has one line per disk, with its model, size, read
and write throughput, the share of time it was busy, the mean time an
operation took, and its temperature when the drive reports one.
Loop, RAM and zram devices are not listed, nor are disks attached
after Dials started.  Choose a disk with @key{UP} and @key{DOWN}.

Below the list are the details of the chosen disk and graphs of its
reads, writes and busy time.  The throughput graphs scale to the
highest rate seen, so that small transfers still show.

The table at the bottom lists the file systems on local disks with
their size, the space used and free, and a bar of their use.  A device
mounted in several places, as Btrfs subvolumes often are, is listed
once.  Network file systems are left out, because measuring one that
does not answer would stop the program.

@node Network page
@section The Network page
@cindex Network page
@cindex interfaces

The list at the top has one line per network interface: its state,
link speed, the rates at which it receives and sends, and the bytes
received and sent since it came up.  Interfaces backed by hardware
come first, then virtual ones such as bridges and tunnels, and the
loopback last.  Interfaces that appear or go away while Dials runs
are added and removed.  Choose one with @key{UP} and @key{DOWN}.

Below are the chosen interface's hardware address, MTU, packet rates,
errors and drops, and its addresses, followed by graphs of what it
receives and sends.  The last line counts TCP connections by state
and UDP sockets.

@node Processes page
@section The Processes page
@cindex Processes page
@cindex signals, sending
@cindex nice value

The first line counts the tasks by state and their threads.  Below,
one line per process gives its PID, user, state, nice value, threads,
share of a processor, share of memory, memory used, processor time
and command.  Kernel threads, which have no command line, are shown
by name in brackets.

@table @kbd
@item @key{UP}
@itemx @key{DOWN}
@itemx @key{PgUp}
@itemx @key{PgDn}
Choose a process.  The choice follows the process as the list is
sorted again.
@item F5
Sort by processor use, memory, PID, user, name or processor time, in
turn.
@item F6
Show the processes as a tree, each under its parent, or as a list.
@item /
Show only the processes whose name or command contains some text,
ignoring case.  An empty text shows all again.
@item F7
Change the chosen process's nice value.  Raising it is allowed for
your own processes; lowering it needs root privileges.
@item F8
Send a signal to the chosen process: TERM to ask it to end, KILL to
end it at once, HUP, INT, STOP to pause it or CONT to let it continue.
Nothing is sent until you press @key{RET}.
@end table

On this page, F7 and F8 do these instead of their usual jobs; use
@kbd{r} to clear the history.  With @option{--demo}, no signal is
sent and no nice value changed.

@node Sensors page
@section The Sensors page
@cindex Sensors page
@cindex hardware monitoring

The table lists every temperature, fan, voltage, current and power
input of the hardware monitoring drivers, grouped by chip, with its
reading now, the lowest and highest seen, and the limit the driver
gives.  Chips that share a driver, such as one per memory module, are
numbered.  Readings are colored by how near they are to their limit.
Choose a sensor with @key{UP}, @key{DOWN}, @key{PgUp} and @key{PgDn}
to see its history in the graph below.

@node Power page
@section The Power page
@cindex Power page
@cindex batteries
@cindex RAPL

The page lists batteries: the system's own, with its charge, state,
time left, rate, health against its design capacity, charge cycles
and voltage, and those of devices such as a wireless mouse, which
often report only a level such as @samp{Normal}.  Mains and USB power
supplies show whether they are connected.

Processor power comes from RAPL, where the hardware has it; most
systems let only root read it, and the page says so.  Power readings
from hardware monitoring, such as those of a power supply, are listed
too.  The graph shows the processor package's power, or else the
first power reading, or else the battery's charge.

