@node Settings
@chapter Settings
@cindex settings
@cindex configuration file
@cindex @file{dials.conf}

Choose @samp{Save setup} in the Options menu to keep the sampling
interval, the color theme, the panels and their pages, and how the
Processes page sorts its list, for the next time Dials starts.  They
are written to @file{dials/dials.conf} under the directory named by
the environment variable @env{XDG_CONFIG_HOME}, or under
@file{~/.config} if it is not set.  Options given on the command line
take precedence.

The file holds lines of @samp{@var{name} = @var{value}}, which may be
edited; lines starting with @samp{#} are ignored, and Dials names
those it does not understand.  For example:

@example
interval = 2
theme = classic
left-page = overview
right-page = gpu
active-panel = left
two-panels = yes
process-sort = cpu
process-tree = no
@end example

@node Logging
@chapter Logging
@cindex logging
@cindex CSV
@cindex JSON

Press @kbd{l}, or choose @samp{Start logging} in the Options menu, to
write each sample to a file; the menu bar shows @samp{REC} meanwhile.
The file is the one given with @option{--log}, or else a new file
named after the date and time, such as
@file{dials-20261119-142500.csv}, in the working directory.  A file
that exists is added to.

A file whose name ends in @file{.json} or @file{.jsonl} gets one JSON
object per line; any other gets comma-separated values with a heading
line.  Each line has the time, processor use, memory and swap in MiB,
the load average, the temperature, utilization, memory and power of
each GPU, the bytes read and written per second on each disk, and the
bytes received and sent per second on each hardware network
interface.  Numbers always use @samp{.} as the decimal point.  Values
that cannot be read are left empty, or are @code{null} in JSON.  The
columns are fixed when logging starts.

@node Sources
@chapter Where the measurements come from
@cindex sources of measurements
@cindex @file{/proc}
@cindex @file{/sys}

Dials reads what the Linux kernel publishes; it does not need a
daemon or a kernel module of its own.  These are the main sources:

@table @asis
@item The system
@file{/proc/sys/kernel/hostname}, @file{/proc/sys/kernel/osrelease},
@file{/etc/os-release}, @file{/proc/cpuinfo} and @file{/proc/uptime}.

@item Processors
@file{/proc/stat}, @file{/proc/loadavg}, @file{/proc/pressure/cpu},
@file{/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpufreq} and the hardware
monitoring drivers in @file{/sys/class/hwmon}.

@item Memory
@file{/proc/meminfo}, @file{/proc/vmstat}, @file{/proc/pressure/memory},
@file{/sys/block/zram*/mm_stat}, and @file{/proc/@var{pid}/statm} for
the processes using the most.

@item Graphics processors
@file{/sys/class/drm/card*/device} and its hardware monitoring
directory, or NVML from @file{libnvidia-ml.so.1} when Dials was
configured with @option{--enable-nvml}, and the PCI ID database in @file{/usr/share/hwdata/pci.ids}
or @file{/usr/share/misc/pci.ids}.

@item Disks
@file{/sys/block}, @file{/proc/diskstats} and @file{/proc/self/mounts},
and @code{statvfs} for the space of each file system.

@item Network
@file{/proc/net/dev}, @file{/sys/class/net}, @file{/proc/net/tcp},
@file{/proc/net/tcp6}, @file{/proc/net/udp} and @file{/proc/net/udp6},
and @code{getifaddrs} for the addresses.

@item Processes
@file{/proc/@var{pid}/stat}, @file{/proc/@var{pid}/comm} and
@file{/proc/@var{pid}/cmdline}; the owner of @file{/proc/@var{pid}}
is the process's user.

@item Sensors
@file{/sys/class/hwmon}.

@item Power
@file{/sys/class/power_supply} and @file{/sys/class/powercap}.
@end table

Most of these files are kept open and read again in place at each
sample, which is much cheaper than opening them again.  The
@option{--sysroot} option reads the same files under another directory
(@pxref{Invoking dials}).

@node Reporting bugs
@chapter Reporting bugs
@cindex bugs, reporting

Please include the output of @samp{dials --version} and
@samp{dials --once} in a bug report, together with the name of the
terminal you use.

